					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/fuzzyboots" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1229418); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1229418&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609254575" alt="FuzzyBoots" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1229418)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/fuzzyboots" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1229418); return false;" >FuzzyBoots</a></span> <span class="odate time_1332450986 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">22 Mar 2012 21:16</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1400227">
									

<p>I kind of like the idea of it, but it does feel a bit bare. The description of the film's contents suddenly ends with an intertitle. Is that all the film that's there? If not, I feel like the researchers would indicate something about what happens after (if it's just blank film, darkness, but they'd note that). Also, it feels a bit odd for them to stop with &quot;if you watch all of the film, you're sucked in&quot;. Given the way the Foundation is generally shown trying to experiment, I'd prefer to see something along the lines of &quot;After watching approximately 11.3 minutes of the movie's footage, the subject is transported to the movie. Note to researchers, this is 11.3 cumulative minutes. Repeated exposure will result in adverse effects&quot; or &quot;If the entirety of the twelfth segment is viewed, the viewer is transported into the movie&quot;. Also, it's fairly standard in objects which do something when you observe them that the Foundation notes whether recordings have the same effect, if viewing it through a camera has the same effect, and whether it affects people with given vision issues.</p>
<p>I do like the description of the surreal imagery, although the nature of the film seems to be somewhat self-limiting. Would it work if every group sucked in on a viewing created a new segment with additional film? That would produce a reason to continue studying the film (presumably using D-class or viewing it via camera if such removes the effect) to try to determine who the trapped people are.</p>

							</div>

								
					
